t\\tSELECT p.id, p.name, p.description, p.picture, \\n\\t\\t       p.price_currency_code, p.price_units, p.price_nanos, p.categories\\n\\t\\tFROM catalog.products p\\n\\t\\tWHERE p.id = $1\\n\\t\'}, {\'key\': \'unsanitized_span_name\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'sql.conn.query\'}, {\'key\': \'span.kind\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'client\'}, {\'key\': \'internal.span.format\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'otlp\'}], \'logs\': [], \'processID\': \'p8\', \'warnings\': None}, {\'traceID\': \'0600d1911cac33519dd7a63224d02c29\', \'spanID\': \'99304207cbeab289\', \'operationName\': \'oteldemo.ProductCatalogService/GetProduct\', \'references\': [{\'refType\': \'CHILD_OF\', \'traceID\': \'0600d1911cac33519dd7a63224d02c29\', \'spanID\': \'1178554f6357b88e\'}], \'startTime\': 1773759045096151, \'duration\': 1485, \'tags\': [{\'key\': \'rpc.service\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'oteldemo.ProductCatalogService\'}, {\'key\': \'rpc.method\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'GetProduct\'}, {\'key\': \'rpc.system\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'grpc\'}, {\'key\': \'app.product.id\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'6E92ZMYYFZ\'}, {\'key\': \'app.product.name\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'Solar Filter\'}, {\'key\': \'server.address\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'10.244.2.43\'}, {\'key\': \'server.port\', \'type\': \'int64\', \'value\': 46746}, {\'key\': \'rpc.grpc.status_code\', \'type\': \'int64\', \'value\': 0}, {\'key\': \'span.kind\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'server\'}, {\'key\': \'internal.span.format\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'otlp\'}], \'logs\': [{\'timestamp\': 1773759045097491, \'fields\': [{\'key\': \'event\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'Product Found\'}]}], \'processID\': \'p9\', \'warnings\': [\'clock skew adjustment disabled; not applying calculated delta of -2.075941ms\']}, {\'traceID\': \'0600d1911cac33519dd7a63224d02c29\', \'spanID\': \'5f5819c88d12f7f2\', \'operationName\': \'postgresql\', \'references\': [{\'refType\': \'CHILD_OF\', \'traceID\': \'0600d1911cac33519dd7a63224d02c29\', \'spanID\': \'224c0853ab899a63\'}], \'startTime\': 1773759045111749, \'duration\': 1536, \'tags\': [{\'key\': \'db.system.name\', \'type\': \'string\', \'value\': \'postgresql\'}, {\'key\': \'db.query.text\', \'type\': \'string\',', id='b78a5afb-81fa-425c-9a5a-2993667f7f92', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8b57affe98356cd7'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='800c9204-3bf6-4d21-83bf-0dcca825089e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a problem in the Hotel Reservation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve used the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to list the services in the namespace, get_dependency_graph to see how services interact, and get_traces to look into the frontend service\'s traces. \n\nLooking at the traces for the frontend, there\'s a lot of activity, but all the status codes are 200, so no immediate errors there. However, there\'s a mention of a feature flag evaluation for \'recommendationCacheFailure\' with the result \'off\'. That might indicate that the cache is not being used, but why would that be a problem?\n\nThe dependency graph showed high call counts for \'frontend\' and \'cart\', which could mean those services are under pressure. The traces also show multiple calls to \'product-catalog\' and \'recommendation\' services. Wait, in the traces, there are several spans for \'oteldemo.ProductCatalogService/GetProduct\' with varying durations. Some of them have durations around 21280 microseconds, which seems a bit long. Maybe there\'s a latency issue with the product catalog service?\n\nAlso, looking back at the kubectl get services output, there\'s a \'product-catalog\' service. If that service is having issues, it could cause delays. The user should check the status of the product-catalog deployment and pods to see if there are any failures or scaling issues. They might need to use kubectl describe or get pods to check the health of those services. \n\nAnother angle: the traces show calls to \'recommendation\' service, which might be related to the \'recommendation\' microservice. If that\'s not responding correctly, it could affect the frontend. But the traces for the frontend show successful responses. Maybe the problem is elsewhere, like in the database calls. The traces include PostgreSQL queries, but they all seem to be successful with status code 0. \n\nWait, the user hasn\'t checked the actual pods and deployments yet. They listed the services but didn\'t look into the underlying pods or deployments. Maybe some pods are crashing or not running, leading to retries or increased latency. The next step should be to check the pods related to the high-traffic services like \'frontend\', \'product-catalog\', \'recommendation\', and \'cart\' to see their status and any errors. Using kubectl get pods in the namespace and checking their logs could reveal issues like crashes, restarts, or resource constraints. \n\nAlso, the feature flag \'recommendationCacheFailure\' being off might mean that the cache is disabled, leading to more direct database calls. If the database is under strain, that could cause delays. But the traces don\'t show any database errors. Alternatively, maybe the \'product-catalog\' service is having issues with its dependencies, like the MongoDB instance. The user should check the MongoDB services (mongodb-profile, mongodb-rate, etc.) for any problems. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the pods in the hotel-reservation namespace and check their status. If any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff or similar states, that\'s a red flag. Then, they can look into the logs of those pods to find the root cause. Alternatively, check the deployments to see if there are any rollout issues or scaling problems. \n\nAnother possibility is resource limits. If the pods are being throttled due to CPU or memory limits, that could cause latency. Using kubectl top pods might show if any services are hitting their resource limits. \n\nBut first, let\'s check the pods. So the tool to use here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation".\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The traces show high activity in the frontend and product-catalog services, with potential latency issues in database calls. To identify if any pods are failing or experiencing resource constraints, I need to check the pod status in the hotel-reservation namespace. This will reveal if any microservices are crashing, restarting, or under resource pressure, which could explain the observed behavior.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 865, 'prompt_tokens': 10282, 'total_tokens': 11147, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc47-73a6-7103-a665-813aaf3a8ac8-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 10282, 'output_tokens': 865, 'total_tokens': 11147}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7d5dcc42-26b6-4907-861d-ee365ae88c07'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a hotel reservation application with multiple microservices. They\'ve already used some tools to get an overview of the services, dependency graph, and traces. Now, they\'re looking into the traces for the frontend service and noticed some database calls. The next step is to check the pod status in the hotel-reservation namespace.\n\nThe reasoning here is that if there are any failing pods or resource issues, the kubectl command will show that. So, the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation". This will list all the pods in the namespace, allowing the user to see if any are in a CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or other problematic states. It\'s a crucial step to check the health of the individual pods that make up the microservices.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b6bb7b3393b1b211', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 214, 'prompt_tokens': 11272, 'total_tokens': 11486, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc47-a096-7182-bf9d-d26dadb54625-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b6bb7b3393b1b211', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11272, 'output_tokens': 214, 'total_tokens': 11486}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS             RESTARTS        AGE\naux-service-db6bf9895-8ltv8               0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   4 (7s ago)      72s\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-vmkln                   1/1     Running            0               3m39s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-zqqxv                 1/1     Running            0               3m38s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-x2n9h                      1/1     Running            2 (2m46s ago)   3m38s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-h7pqx                   1/1     Running            0               3m35s\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-mw6cm         1/1     Running            0               3m32s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-xgnmg            1/1     Running            0               3m28s\nmemcached-reserve-68dbb57476-5rzcw        1/1     Running            0               3m16s\nmongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-s8zww               1/1     Running            0               3m36s\nmongodb-profile-775dfd4bd7-khp8g          1/1     Running            0               3m31s\nmongodb-rate-bfbcf4587-54dl9              1/1     Running            0               3m26s\nmongodb-recommendation-674dddbcdf-9jp4j   1/1     Running            0               3m21s\nmongodb-reservation-6cdf69dcc-fz4rh       1/1     Running            0               3m13s\nmongodb-user-6dcf4877-fstbb               1/1     Running            0               3m5s\nprofile-69cb98cb67-sp9bw                  1/1     Running            2               3m30s\nrate-6fff88f6f5-7vpn8                     1/1     Running            2 (2m10s ago)   3m24s\nrecommendation-64754c9c4f-p5wsh           1/1     Running            2 (2m7s ago)    3m18s\nreservation-7bb77b596b-mnkdt              1/1     Running            2 (117s ago)    3m11s\nsearch-5f668579c9-fjghh                   1/1     Running            0               3m7s\nuser-6548d868cd-dnwpc                     1/1     Running            0               3m3s\nwrk2-job-7f6rg                            1/1     Running            0               94s\n', id='f1e9f32a-9a90-4fd4-9e78-3866e3972e81', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b6bb7b3393b1b211'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='de546717-c14e-478f-856c-c5150beb7a67'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the hotel-reservation application. So far, they've checked the services and pods. The traces showed some activity in frontend and product-catalog, with some database calls. Then they checked the pods and found that aux-service and profile, rate, recommendation, reservation have restarts. \n\nWait, the aux-service is in CrashLoopBackOff, which means it's crashing repeatedly. Similarly, profile has 2 restarts, rate has 2, recommendation has 2, reservation has 2. Those could be problematic. But the user needs to find the root cause.\n\nThe next step is to check why these pods are crashing. For aux-service, maybe there's a configuration error or a dependency issue. Since aux-service is a service, maybe it's dependent on another service that's not running properly. Also, the traces showed some database calls, maybe the aux-service is trying to connect to a database that's down or has issues.\n\nBut the user has to use the available tools. The tools include kubectl commands, so maybe they should check the logs of the failing pods. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used to get logs. For example, checking the logs of aux-service to see why it's crashing. Also, checking the logs of the other pods with restarts to see if there are any errors.\n\nAlternatively, they could check the deployments to see if there are any rollout issues. But the user hasn't checked deployments yet. However, the available tool is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd, so they can use that to get logs. Let me check the available tools again. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run commands like logs. So the next step is to get logs for the pods that are crashing, like aux-service, profile, rate, etc. That would help identify the specific error messages or issues causing the crashes.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The pod status shows several services (aux-service, profile, rate, recommendation, reservation) have restarts or are in CrashLoopBackOff. To identify the root cause, I need to inspect the logs of these failing pods to detect errors, crashes, or misconfigurations. This will reveal specific issues like dependency failures, resource exhaustion, or code errors causing the restarts.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 483, 'prompt_tokens': 11187, 'total_tokens': 11670, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc47-b082-7972-a1cf-63d4525a63c4-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11187, 'output_tokens': 483, 'total_tokens': 11670}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='998d2d46-03b8-4f98-9579-da753ff74cdd'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their hotel reservation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve used kubectl to list services and pods, and found some pods in CrashLoopBackOff or with restarts. The next step is to check the logs of those failing pods to find out why they\'re crashing.\n\nThe user has already identified that aux-service, profile, rate, recommendation, and reservation pods have issues. The next logical step is to get the logs for these pods. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with the logs command makes sense here. They need to specify the pod names and the container names if there are multiple containers. But since the user hasn\'t specified containers yet, maybe they should start by getting the logs for the pods that are crashing. \n\nWait, the command needs to start with "kubectl". The available commands include "kubectl logs". So the correct command would be "kubectl logs <pod-name> -n hotel-reservation". But since there are multiple pods, maybe they should check each one individually. However, the user might want to check all the problematic pods at once. But the tool allows execut