the user didn\'t provide the output of that command. Wait, in the previous step, the user tried to get the pods\' container ports but maybe the output wasn\'t shown. Wait, the user\'s last tool call was to get services, which shows aux-service on 8080. But the pod is crashing because it can\'t bind. So perhaps the aux-service\'s pod is configured to use port 8080, but another service is already using that port. \n\nWait, but in Kubernetes, services are abstract and don\'t directly bind to ports on the node. The pods themselves have container ports. So if two pods are trying to use the same container port, that\'s a conflict. So the user needs to check which pods have a container port set to 8080. \n\nThe user previously tried to get the pods\' container ports with kubectl get pods -o jsonpath=\'{.items[*].spec.containers[*].ports}\' but maybe that command\'s output wasn\'t provided. Wait, in the history, the user ran that command and the response was a JSON path, but the actual output wasn\'t shown. Wait, no, the user\'s last tool call was to get services, and the response was the services list. \n\nWait, maybe the user needs to check the container ports again. Let me think: the aux-service pod is trying to start a server on port 8080, but another pod is already using that port. So the user should check all pods in the namespace for any container that has a port 8080. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service\'s service is configured to use port 8080, but the pod is also trying to expose that port, leading to a conflict. But in Kubernetes, the service\'s port is separate from the container\'s port. Wait, no. The service\'s port is the port that the service exposes, and the container\'s port is the port the container listens on. So if the aux-service\'s pod is configured to listen on port 8080 (container port), and the service is mapped to that port, then another pod can\'t use the same container port. \n\nSo the user needs to check all pods in the namespace to see if any have a container port set to 8080. If another pod is using port 8080, that\'s the conflict. \n\nBut the user already ran kubectl get pods -o jsonpath to get container ports. Let me check the previous steps. The user\'s last tool call was to get services, which shows aux-service on 8080. But the pod is crashing. So maybe the aux-service\'s pod is the only one using port 8080, but the error suggests that another pod is using it. \n\nWait, the error message is from the aux-service pod\'s log: "Address already in use" when trying to bind to port 8080. That means another process in the same pod is using that port, or another pod in the same namespace is using the same port. \n\nWait, but the pod is the only one using that port. Unless there\'s a service that\'s exposing that port, but that\'s not the case here. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service\'s pod is configured to use port 8080, but the service is set to a different port. Wait, the service for aux-service is ClusterIP with port 8080/TCP. So the service is pointing to the pod\'s container port 8080. \n\nBut if the pod itself is trying to bind to port 8080, and another pod is also using that port, that\'s a conflict. \n\nSo the user needs to check all pods in the namespace for any container that has a port 8080. If another pod has a container port 8080, that\'s the issue. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t checked that yet. The previous tool call was to get services, which shows aux-service on 8080. So maybe the user should run the kubectl get pods -o jsonpath command again to check for container ports. \n\nWait, the user previously tried to run that command but the output wasn\'t provided. Wait, in the history, the user\'s last tool call was to get services, and the response was the services list. So the user hasn\'t yet checked the container ports. \n\nTherefore, the next step is to run kubectl get pods -o jsonpath=\'{.items[*].spec.containers[*].ports}\' to list all container ports across all pods. This will show if any pod has a container port set to 8080, which would cause the conflict. \n\nOnce that\'s done, the user can identify which pod is using port 8080 and resolve the conflict by changing the port in the pod\'s configuration or the service\'s port mapping. \n\nSo the correct tool to use now is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command to get the container ports.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: The aux-service pod is failing due to a port conflict on port 8080. To confirm if another pod is using this port, I need to check all container ports across the namespace. This will reveal if any other service is inadvertently using port 8080, causing the conflict. The solution likely involves adjusting port configurations in the pod or service definitions. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation -o jsonpath=\'{.items[*].spec.containers[*].ports}\'"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1495, 'prompt_tokens': 12729, 'total_tokens': 14224, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc48-41c1-7811-b2b6-112e030016f4-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12729, 'output_tokens': 1495, 'total_tokens': 14224}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0a298d76-8312-4138-9c3d-8cee297998a0'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to figure out why the aux-service pod is crashing. The logs show an OSError about port 8080 being already in use. So the first thought is that another service is using port 8080, causing the aux-service to fail when it tries to bind to it.\n\nEarlier, they checked the services in the namespace and saw that aux-service is set to use port 8080. But maybe another pod is also using the same port. The user ran a command to get all container ports across the namespace. Let me check the output of that command.\n\nWait, the output from the kubectl get pods command with the jsonpath for ports would list all the ports each container is using. If another pod has a container configured to use port 8080, that's the conflict. For example, if the frontend service is using port 5000, but maybe another service like profile or rate is using 8080. \n\nLooking at the services list, the frontend is on 5000, profile on 8081, rate on 8084, recommendation on 8085, reservation on 8087, etc. So aux-service is the only one on 8080. But maybe a pod's container is set to use 8080 even if the service is on a different port. Or perhaps there's a pod that's not listed in the services, like a job or a sidecar container.\n\nWait, the user also checked the pods and saw that aux-service is in CrashLoopBackOff. The logs indicate the port is already in use. So the next step is to check all the containers in all pods to see if any of them have a port 8080. The previous command to get the ports might have shown that. If another container is using 8080, that's the culprit. For example, maybe the frontend pod has a container that's using 8080, but the service is mapped to 5000. Or maybe a different service's container is using 8080.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service's own container is configured to use 8080, but another pod's container is also using 8080. The user needs to check all the ports in all containers. If the output from the kubectl command shows that another container is using 8080, then that's the conflict. If not, maybe there's a different issue, like a misconfigured service or a problem with the node's port allocation.\n\nSo the next step is to run the command to list all container ports. If that shows another container using 8080, then the solution is to change the port in aux-service's configuration. If not, maybe the service is correctly configured, but there's another reason, like a firewall rule or a node-level issue. But given the logs, the most likely cause is a port conflict, so checking the container ports is the right move.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation -o jsonpath=\'{.items[*].spec.containers[*].ports}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ae0c1368bc64309f', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 691, 'prompt_tokens': 13769, 'total_tokens': 14460, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc48-8d1e-75f2-ada0-73a080c6c3d0-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].ports}'"}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ae0c1368bc64309f', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 13769, 'output_tokens': 691, 'total_tokens': 14460}), ToolMessage(content='[{"containerPort":8080,"name":"http","protocol":"TCP"}] [{"containerPort":8300,"protocol":"TCP"},{"containerPort":8400,"protocol":"TCP"},{"containerPort":8500,"protocol":"TCP"},{"containerPort":53,"protocol":"UDP"}] [{"containerPort":5000,"protocol":"TCP"}] [{"containerPort":8083,"protocol":"TCP"}] [{"containerPort":14269,"protocol":"TCP"},{"containerPort":5778,"protocol":"TCP"},{"containerPort":14268,"protocol":"TCP"},{"containerPort":14267,"protocol":"TCP"},{"containerPort":16686,"protocol":"TCP"},{"containerPort":5775,"protocol":"UDP"},{"containerPort":6831,"protocol":"UDP"},{"containerPort":6832,"p