ssage says the port is already in use, so there must be another process using it.\n\nWait, maybe the aux-service\'s container is trying to bind to port 8080, but another pod\'s container is also using that port. For example, if there\'s another service in the same namespace that\'s using port 8080. But the user\'s kubectl get services might show that. Wait, the user hasn\'t checked the services yet. The previous step only checked the container ports, not the services. So maybe the service is configured to use port 8080, and the container is also using it, leading to a conflict. Or another service is using the same port.\n\nSo the next step is to check the services in the namespace to see if any are using port 8080. The tool get_services can retrieve the list of services in Grafana. If there\'s a service named aux-service or another service using port 8080, that would explain the conflict. Alternatively, the service might be using a different port, but the container is configured to use 8080, leading to a conflict.\n\nTherefore, the next tool to use is get_services to check if any service in the hotel-reservation namespace is using port 8080. If there\'s a service with port 8080, that\'s the cause. If not, then the problem might be with the container\'s configuration or another pod using the same port. But since the user\'s initial logs indicate the port is in use, checking the services is a logical next step.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_services\nreasoning: To determine if another service in the hotel-reservation namespace is using port 8080, which could be causing the conflict. This will help identify if the port is occupied by a service rather than a pod.\n\ntool_call: {"name": "get_services", "arguments": {}}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 893, 'prompt_tokens': 3296, 'total_tokens': 4189, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc4a-555c-7de2-8d7a-3262621c6dfd-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3296, 'output_tokens': 893, 'total_tokens': 4189}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b5f34faf-9c45-45fe-8bc0-0412da359363'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application where the aux-service pod is crashing because port 8080 is already in use. \n\nFirst, they checked the pods and found that aux-service is in CrashLoopBackOff. The logs mention an OSError about port 8080 being occupied. Then they used the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to list all container ports across pods in the namespace. The output shows that several pods have container ports, including the aux-service's containerPort 8080. But wait, there's another pod (maybe the database) also using port 8080? Or is it possible that the aux-service's container is trying to use the same port as another service?\n\nNext, the user called get_services to check if any service in the hotel-reservation namespace is using port 8080. The response from get_services is a list of services, but the user hasn't provided the actual output yet. Assuming the output includes services like aux-service, maybe another service like a database or API is using port 8080. \n\nWait, the user's previous tool call to get_services might have returned a list of services. If the aux-service is the only one using port 8080, then the conflict might be within the same service's deployment. For example, if the aux-service's deployment has multiple replicas, each trying to bind to port 8080 on the same node, that could cause a conflict. But Kubernetes typically handles port conflicts by not starting the pod if the port is already in use. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service's container is configured to listen on port 8080, but the service is also exposing port 8080, leading to a conflict between the container's listening port and the service's exposed port. That doesn't make sense because the service's port is separate from the container's port. The container's port is specified in the pod's spec, and the service maps that to a cluster IP. So if the container is listening on 8080, and the service is exposing 8080, that should work unless there's another service or pod using the same port. \n\nWait, the user's kubectl get pods output shows that the aux-service has a containerPort of 8080. But if another pod in the same namespace is also using port 8080, that would cause a conflict. However, the get_services call would list services, not pods. So maybe the user needs to check the services to see if any are using port 8080. If the get_services response includes a service named aux-service that's using port 8080, then the conflict might be within the same service's deployment. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service's deployment is configured with a service that's using port 8080, but the container is also trying to listen on the same port. That would be a mistake in the configuration. The solution would be to change the container's port to a different number, like 8081, and update the service's port accordingly. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the services in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if any are using port 8080. If the get_services tool returns a list that includes a service using port 8080, then the user can proceed to adjust the port configuration. If not, the conflict might be due to another pod using the same port, which would require checking the pods again or looking into the application's configuration.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a056d9ca70478562', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 778, 'prompt_tokens': 4389, 'total_tokens': 5167, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc4a-7ef4-74f1-bc8d-ed4ac9128483-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a056d9ca70478562', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4389, 'output_tokens': 778, 'total_tokens': 5167}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'shipping\', \'jaeger-all-in-one\', \'frontend-proxy\', \'checkout\', \'frontend\', \'product-reviews\', \'currency\', \'email\', \'accounting\', \'quote\', \'cart\', \'recommendation\', \'fraud-detection\', \'load-generator\', \'flagd\', \'payment\', \'image-provider\', \'ad\', \'product-catalog\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='24495ed7-e540-4a8b-a7b1-49c88b56f660', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a056d9ca70478562'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7696d677-5cce-4c33-83da-1bae6564de14')]]
