ernatively, maybe the patch command requires a different format, like using a JSON patch document instead of a JSON object.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user should use the --type=merge option or ensure the JSON is correctly structured. Another possibility is that the JSON is missing a comma between elements or has an extra bracket. Let me try to reconstruct the correct JSON.\n\nThe correct JSON for the patch should look like this:\n\n{\n  "spec": {\n    "template": {\n      "spec": {\n        "containers": [\n          {\n            "name": "aux-service",\n            "ports": [\n              {\n                "containerPort": 8081,\n                "name": "http",\n                "protocol": "TCP"\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nBut when using kubectl patch, the -p flag expects a JSON patch document. However, the user might be using a JSON object instead of a JSON patch. Alternatively, maybe the JSON is not properly formatted with commas. Let me check the original command again.\n\nIn the user\'s command, after the "ports" array, there\'s a closing bracket for the container, then another closing bracket for the containers array. Wait, the containers array is [ { ... } ], so that\'s correct. Then the spec is { "spec": { ... } }, which is correct. But maybe the JSON is missing a comma after the "ports" array. Wait, no, the structure is correct. \n\nWait, the error message says "invalid character \'}\' after array element". That suggests that there\'s an extra \'}\' somewhere. Let me check the user\'s JSON again. The user\'s JSON ends with \'}\' after the containers array, which is correct. But maybe the JSON is not properly escaped when passed as a string. For example, in the command, the JSON is enclosed in single quotes, and the user has to ensure that the quotes are properly escaped. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user should use a JSON patch format, which uses operations like "replace". For example:\n\n{\n  "sp