y has one element, which is a container with ports array. The ports array has one element. Each of these should be properly closed. Maybe the problem is that the last } is closing the wrong level. Let me restructure the JSON to ensure each level is correctly closed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the issue is that the JSON is not properly formatted with commas between elements. Let me ensure that each key-value pair is separated by a comma, except for the last one. Wait, in the ports array, the last element doesn\'t need a comma after it. But in the containers array, the same applies. Let me check that.\n\nAnother possibility: the JSON is missing a comma between some elements. For example, between the name and ports in the container. Wait, the container object has "name": "aux-service", then "ports": [...]. There\'s a comma after the name, which is correct. The ports array is correctly formatted.\n\nHmm, maybe the problem is that the JSON is too deeply nested, causing the parser to fail. Let me try simplifying the JSON. Instead of using multiple nested objects, maybe use a more straightforward structure. But I need to target the correct path in the deployment.\n\nWait, the correct patch should target the containers\' ports. The path is spec.template.spec.containers.0.ports.0.containerPort. So the JSON should set that value to 8081. Let me structure the JSON accordingly, ensuring that each level is properly closed.\n\nLet me try rewriting the JSON with proper nesting and braces. Maybe the original command had an extra brace at the end. Let me remove the last } and see if that fixes the error. Alternatively, ensure that all braces are properly matched.\n\nAfter careful review, I realize that the JSON might have an extra closing brace. Let me adjust the command to remove the redundant brace and ensure the structure is correct. This should resolve the syntax error and allow the patch to apply successfully.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: The previou