tool_call: {"command": "kubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p '{\"spec\":{\"template\":{\"spec\":{\"containers\":[{\"name\":\"aux-service\",\"ports\":[{\"containerPort\":8081,\"name\":\"http\",\"protocol\":\"TCP\"}]}}]}}'"} - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='22581616-d892-496a-8303-6699b7f6de22'), HumanMessage(content='You will be working this application:\n\nHotel Reservation\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nhotel-reservation\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application that\'s having issues. The main problem seems to be with the aux-service pod, which is crashing because port 8080 is already in use. \n\nFirst, the user checked the pods and found that aux-service is in CrashLoopBackOff. The logs show an OSError about port 8080 being occupied. Then they checked the services in the namespace and saw that aux-service is set to use port 8080. But when they looked at the container ports across all pods, they found that the aux-service\'s container is using port 8080. \n\nWait, but why is there a conflict if only aux-service is using that port? Maybe another pod is using the same port. The user ran a command to list all container ports, and the output showed that the aux-service has a containerPort of 8080. But if there\'s another pod using the same port, that would cause the conflict. However, looking at the services list, no other service is on 8080. \n\nHmm, maybe the aux-service is a deployment with multiple replicas. If one replica is already running and using port 8080, another replica trying to start would conflict. But the user\'s kubectl get pods shows only one pod for aux-service, which is in CrashLoopBackOff. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service\'s container is configured to listen on port 8080, but the service is also exposing port 8080. That would mean the container is trying to listen on the same port as the service, causing a conflict. But in Kubernetes, the service\'s port is separate from the container\'s port. The container\'s port is specified in the pod\'s spec, and the service maps that to a cluster IP. So if the container is listening on port 8080, and the service is exposing port 8080, that should work. Unless the container is trying to listen on the same port as the service, which would be a mistake. \n\nWait, the logs show that the aux-service is trying to start a server on port 8080, but it\'s already in use. The only possible explanation is that another service or pod is using port 8080. But according to the services list, no other service is using that port. \n\nMaybe the aux-service is using a port that\'s already allocated by another service. For example, if another service is using port 8080, but it\'s not listed. But the user checked the services and didn\'t find any. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service is using a port that\'s already in use by another container in the same pod. But the pod has only one container. \n\nThis is confusing. The user\'s logs indicate a port conflict on 8080, but the services list doesn\'t show any other service using that port. The only possible explanation is that the aux-service\'s own container is trying to use the same port, which would be a mistake in the configuration. \n\nSo the root cause is that the aux-service is configured to use port 8080, but another service or pod is already occupying this port, leading to the conflict. The solution is to change the port configuration in the aux-service\'s deployment to use an unused port, like 8081, to resolve the conflict.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the failure is a **port conflict on port 8080**. The `aux-service` is configured to use port 8080, but another service or pod is already occupying this port, leading to the "Address already in use" error. This prevents the `aux-service` from starting, resulting in the `CrashLoopBackOff` state. \n\n### Key Findings:\n1. **Port Conflict**: The `aux-service` pod fails to bind to port 8080 because another process (likely another pod or service) is already using it.\n2. **Service Configuration**: The `aux-service` service is set to expose port 8080, but the container\'s application is also attempting to listen on the same port, causing a conflict.\n3. **Resolution**: Adjust the `aux-service` deployment to use an unused port (e.g., 8081) to avoid the conflict.\n\n### Recommended Action:\n- **Modify the `aux-service` deployment** to use a different port (e.g., 8081) in both the container configuration and the service definition.\n- **Verify** that no other service or pod is using the new port before redeploying. \n\nThis change will resolve the port conflict and allow the `aux-service` to start successfully.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='353b0b5f-8fa3-44a1-a882-8ed950a8373f'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='fb736b3d-a272-4184-8a87-69a5da339c79'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by understanding the problem. The user mentioned that the aux-service pod is crashing due to port 8080 being in use. My first thought is to check what services are running in the namespace to see if another service is using port 8080. The get_services tool can help list all services in Grafana, which might show if another service is on the same port.\n\nWait, but the user already checked the services and found that aux-service is the only one on port 8080. Hmm, maybe there\'s a conflict within the same service. Maybe the deployment has multiple replicas, and the port is being used by another replica. Or perhaps the container itself is trying to bind to the same port as the service. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service\'s container is configured to listen on port 8080, but the service is also exposing port 8080. That would caus