k the service\'s port configuration to make sure it\'s also updated. If the service is still on 8080, that could cause another conflict. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user should use kubectl apply to update the deployment YAML, which might be more reliable. But since the user is restricted to non-interactive commands, using patch is the way to go. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to run the patch command again, ensuring the JSON is correctly formatted. Then, check the service\'s port configuration using get_services or another tool. If the service\'s port is still 8080, adjust that as well.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: The previous patch command did not update the container port to 8081. I need to reapply the patch with corrected JSON formatting to ensure the containerPort is set to 8081. This will resolve the port conflict.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{\\"spec\\":{\\"template\\":{\\"spec\\":{\\"containers\\":[{\\"name\\":\\"aux-service\\",\\"ports\\":[{\\"containerPort\\":8081,\\"name\\":\\"http\\",\\"protocol\\":\\"TCP\\"}]}}]}}}\'"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 786, 'prompt_tokens': 4017, 'total_tokens': 4803, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc4b-3f5e-7531-8734-9260063646eb-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4017, 'output_tokens': 786, 'total_tokens': 4803}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e02f7a10-4443-458f-80ed-56523b20869a'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is trying to fix a port conflict on port 8080 for the aux-service in their Kubernetes cluster. They initially tried to edit the deployment but got rejected because interactive commands aren\'t allowed. Then they tried patching the deployment to change the container port to 8081, but the response showed the port was still 8080. \n\nHmm, maybe the patch command wasn\'t formatted correctly. The user tried again with a corrected JSON structure, but the tool response still shows 8080. Wait, perhaps the issue is that the patch isn\'t targeting the right part of the deployment. Let me check the JSON structure they\'re using.\n\nThe command is: \nkubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"aux-service","ports":[{"containerPort":8081,"name":"http","protocol":"TCP"}]}]}}}}\'\n\nWait, the JSON might be missing quotes or having syntax errors. Let me verify the JSON structure. Oh, right! The user might have missed enclosing the entire JSON in double quotes. The previous attempt had single quotes, which might not be valid. Also, the patch command needs to be properly formatted with escaped quotes.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the deployment\'s container port is set in a different part of the spec. For example, maybe the ports are defined under a different key or the container name is incorrect. The user should confirm the exact container name and structure. \n\nAnother approach: use kubectl get deployment to check the current configuration. But the user already tried getting the current port and saw 8080. So the patch should work if the JSON is correct. \n\nWait, in the tool response after the first patch, the port was still 8080. That suggests the patch didn\'t apply correctly. Maybe the JSON syntax was incorrect. Let me check the JSON again. The user\'s command uses single quotes for the JSON, but the tool might expect double quotes. Also, the JSON structure might need to be properly escaped. \n\nThe correct command should use double quotes and ensure the JSON is valid. For example:\nkubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"aux-service","ports":[{"containerPort":8081,"name":"http","protocol":"TCP"}]}]}}}}\'\n\nBut the user\'s previous attempt had single quotes, which might not be valid. So the next step is to reapply the patch with the correct JSON formatting, ensuring that the quotes are properly escaped and the structure is correct. If that fails, maybe check the deployment\'s YAML to see where the port is defined and patch that specific section.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{\\"spec\\":{\\"template\\":{\\"spec\\":{\\"containers\\":[{\\"name\\":\\"aux-service\\",\\"ports\\":[{\\"containerPort\\":8081,\\"name\\":\\"http\\",\\"protocol\\":\\"TCP\\"}]}}]}}}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b8a9f2362f29152d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 654, 'prompt_tokens': 5167, 'total_tokens': 5821, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc4b-6422-7a42-b0d4-6853e726c8b7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"aux-service","ports":[{"containerPort":8081,"name":"http","protocol":"TCP"}]}}]}}}\''}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b8a9f2362f29152d', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5167, 'output_tokens': 654, 'total_tokens': 5821}), ToolMessage(content="Dry-run failed. Potentially it's an invalid command. stderr: Error from server (BadRequest): invali