missing a comma after the container in the containers array. Wait, no, because there\'s only one container. Alternatively, maybe the JSON is missing a closing brace after the "ports" array. Let me check:\n\nThe ports array is:\n\n"ports": [\n  {\n    "containerPort": 8081,\n    "name": "http",\n    "protocol": "TCP"\n  }\n]\n\nThis is correctly closed with ] and the } for the container. So the JSON structure seems correct. \n\nWait, maybe the problem is that the JSON is not properly formatted with commas between elements. But in this case, the containers array has only one element, so no comma is needed. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the JSON is missing a closing brace after the "spec" in the template. Let me check again. The "spec" inside the template should have "containers" as an array, which is correctly closed. \n\nHmm, I\'m stuck. Maybe I should try a different approach. Instead of using a patch, I can use a YAML file to update the deployment. But since the user only allows kubectl commands, I need to make sure the JSON is correctly formatted. \n\nWait, perhaps the error is due to an extra closing brace. Let me count the braces in the JSON:\n\n1. "spec": { ... }\n2. "template": { ... }\n3. "spec": { ... }\n4. "containers": [ ... ]\n5. { ... } (container)\n6. ] (closing containers array)\n7. } (closing spec)\n8. } (closing template)\n9. } (closing spec)\n\nThat\'s 9 braces. The JSON should have 9 braces. But the error is about an invalid } after the array element. Maybe the JSON is missing a comma after the container in the containers array. Wait, no, because there\'s only one container. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the JSON is missing a closing brace after the "ports" array. Let me check the ports part again:\n\n"ports": [\n  {\n    "containerPort": 8081,\n    "name": "http",\n    "protocol": "TCP"\n  }\n]\n\nThis is correctly closed with ] and the } for the container. \n\nI think the issue is that the JSON is not properly escaped in the command. Let me try to reformat