e JSON to ensure correct structure and avoid extra closing braces. The goal is to update the container port to 8081 in the `aux-service` deployment.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{\\"spec\\":{\\"template\\":{\\"spec\\":{\\"containers\\":[{\\"name\\":\\"aux-service\\",\\"ports\\":[{\\"containerPort\\":8081,\\"name\\":\\"http\\",\\"protocol\\":\\"TCP\\"}]}}]}}\'"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 2371, 'prompt_tokens': 5468, 'total_tokens': 7839, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc4e-06a7-7eb3-8f8b-988fca3557a0-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5468, 'output_tokens': 2371, 'total_tokens': 7839}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1edcb67c-652e-42da-8c7e-06b4640b3ebb'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out why the JSON is still causing an error. The user has been trying to patch the deployment for the aux-service to change the container port from 8080 to 8081. Each time they attempt to use kubectl patch, they get an error about an invalid closing brace.\n\nFirst, I need to check the JSON structure they\'re using. The command they\'re running is:\n\nkubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{\\"spec\\":{\\"template\\":{\\"spec\\":{\\"containers\\":[{\\"name\\":\\"aux-service\\",\\"ports\\":[{\\"containerPort\\":8081,\\"name\\":\\"http\\",\\"protocol\\":\\"TCP\\"}]}}]}}\'\n\nLooking at this, the JSON seems to be missing the closing brace for the "spec" field. Let me break it down:\n\nThe patch payload starts with {"spec": {...}}, but the structure inside might not be properly closed. Let me visualize the JSON:\n\n{\n  "spec": {\n    "template": {\n      "spec": {\n        "containers": [\n          {\n            "name": "aux-service",\n            "ports": [\n              {\n                "containerPort": 8081,\n                "name": "http",\n                "protocol": "TCP"\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nWait, the original command\'s JSON might be missing the closing brace for "spec" or "template". Let me count the braces:\n\nThe user\'s JSON ends with "}}\'", which might be an extra closing brace. Let me check:\n\nThe command ends with "}}\'", which is two closing braces. That\'s probably the issue. The correct JSON should have one closing brace for "spec" and another for "template", but maybe the structure is not properly nested.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the "containers" array is not properly closed. Let me check the structure again. The "containers" array has one item, which is a container with ports. The ports array has one item. So the JSON should be:\n\n{\n  "spec": {\n    "template": {\n      "spec": {\n        "containers": [\n          {\n            "name": "aux-service",\n            "ports": [\n              {\n                "containerPort": 8081,\n                "name": "http",\n                "protocol": "TCP"\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nBut in the user\'s command, the JSON might be missing a closing brace for "spec" or "template". Let me see the exact string they\'re using:\n\nThe command is: -p \'{\\"spec\\":{\\"template\\":{\\"spec\\":{\\"containers\\":[{\\"name\\":\\"aux-service\\",\\"ports\\":[{\\"containerPort\\":8081,\\"name\\":\\"http\\",\\"protocol\\":\\"TCP\\"}]}}]}}\'\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n- "spec": { ... }\n- "template": { ... }\n- "spec": { ... }\n- "containers": [ ... ]\n- "name": "aux-service", "ports": [ ... ]\n\nWait, the "containers" array is closed with ]}, then the "spec" is closed with }, then the "template" is closed with }, and finally the "spec" is closed with }. But the user\'s JSON ends with "}}\'", which is two closing braces. That\'s probably the issue. The correct JSON should have one closing brace for each level. Let me count:\n\n1. The outermost "spec" should have one closing brace.\n2. Inside "spec", "template" has one closing brace.\n3. Inside "template", "spec" has one closing brace.\n4. Inside "spec", "containers" is an array, which is closed with ].\n5. The "ports" array is closed with ].\n\nSo the correct JSON should end with "}}", not "}}\'". The user\'s command has an extra closing brace, which is causing the error. The solution is to remove the extra closing brace.\n\nTherefore, the correct command should be:\n\nkubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"aux-service","ports":[{"containerPort":8081,"name":"http","protocol":"TCP"}]}]}}}}\'\n\nBut since the user is using a JSON string in the command, they need to ensure that the braces are properly nested. The previous attempts had an extra closing brace, which is causing the "invalid character \'}\' after array element" error. So the fix is to remove that extra brace.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{\\"spec\\":{\\"template\\":{\\"spec\\":{\\"containers\\":[{\\"name\\":\\"aux-service\\",\\"ports\\":[{\\"containerPort\\":8081,\\"name\\":\\"http\\",\\"protocol\\":\\"TCP\\"}]}}]}}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-87d102ea112b55a5', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1009, 'prompt_tokens': 6618, 'total_tokens': 7627, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc4e-7769-7d03-acc7-1a8741b236d1-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl patch deployment aux-service -n hotel-reservation -p \'{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"aux-service","ports":[{"containerPort":8081,"name":"http","protocol":"TCP"}]}}]}}\''}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-87d102ea112b55a5', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6618, 'output_tokens': 1009, 'total_tokens': 7627}), ToolMessage(content="Dry-run failed. Potentially it's an invalid command. stderr: Error from server (BadRequest): invalid character '}' after array element\n", id='e493837f-460d-49f0-8dde-ab2734767fff', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-87d102ea112b55a5'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='65198f80-b02b-4340-bf7a-b5836d3de2bb')]]
