dy in use. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service's container is configured to listen on port 8080, but the service is also exposing port 8080, leading to a conflict between the container's listening port and the service's exposed port. That doesn't make sense because the service's port is separate from the container's port. The container's port is specified in the pod's spec, and the service maps that to a cluster IP. So if the container is listening on 8080, and the service is exposing 8080, that should work unless there's another service or pod using the same port. \n\nWait, the user's kubectl get pods output shows that the aux-service has a containerPort of 8080. But if another pod in the same namespace is also using port 8080, that would cause a conflict. However, the get_services call would list services, not pods. So maybe the user needs to check the services to see if any are using port 8080. If the get_services response includes a service named aux-service that's using port 8080, then the conflict might be within the same service's deployment. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service's deployment is configured with a service that's using port 8080, but the container is also trying to listen on the same port. That would be a mistake in the configuration. The solution would be to change the container's port to a different number, like 8081, and update the service's port accordingly. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the services in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if any are using port 8080. If the get_services tool returns a list that includes a service using port 8080, then the user can proceed to adjust the port configuration. If not, the conflict might be due to another pod using the same port, which would require checking the pods again or looking into the application's configuration.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a056d9ca70478562', 'type': 'functio