However, maybe the service is mapped to a different port. For example, the service might be using port 8080, but the container is using a different port, which is then mapped. Wait, no—the container's port is specified in the pod's spec, and the service maps that to a cluster IP. If the container is listening on 8080, and the service is exposing 8080, that should work. Unless the container is trying to listen on the same port as the service, which would be a mistake. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the aux-service is a deployment with multiple replicas. If one replica is running and using port 8080, another replica trying to start would conflict. But the kubectl get pods shows only one pod for aux-service. \n\nThis is confusing. The logs indicate a port conflict, but the services list doesn't show any other service using 8080. The only possible explanation is that the aux-service's container is trying to use the same port as the service, leading to a conflict. Therefore, changing the container's port to 8081 would resolve the issue. \n\nSo, the next step is to modify the aux-service deployment to use port 8081 in both the container and the service. Then, redeploy and check if the pod starts successfully. If that works, the problem is solved. If not, further investigation is needed.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation -o jsonpath=\'{.items[*].spec.containers[*].ports}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8259c2f30a8504a2', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 744, 'prompt_tokens': 3798, 'total_tokens': 4542, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc4a-2ed5-7642-9eb3-12884980ed37-0', tool_c